    "thesis": {
      "date": "2013-08-10",
      "title": "Optimizing Joint Space Energy of Redundant Tethered Space Robots",
      "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/379478592_Optimizing_Joint_Space_Energy_of_Redundant_Tethered_Space_Robots",
      "abstract": "This research delves into the satellite systems tethered by cables, a study undertaken several decades subsequent to the conceptualization of tethering space systems. Notably, this tethering mechanism finds application in the domain of space elevators as well. The employment of such tethers brings forth considerable advantages for robotic systems governed by extended tethers, prompting their substitution for human counterparts in space missions encompassing repair, equipment installation, and debris elimination from space stations, satellites, and space telescopes. This study focuses on the analysis of a four degrees of freedom tethered space robotic arm, specifically examining how the robot's end effector moves along predefined paths. Algorithms and models are created to describe the robot's setup, including an extended tether acting as an arm that allows the entire robot to move in a circular orbit around Earth. The tether is treated as a rigid, linear arm in mathematical models, and its tension is assessed to determine if the paths are feasible. The robot's movement starts from a platform attached to a spacecraft. Different trajectories, such as direct, oblique, and circular paths, are analyzed, considering historical angle changes in joint variables and the torque needed for these changes. The study calculates the robot's potential energy through gravitational force integration and employs Lagrange equations, kinetic energy, potential energy concepts, equilibrium equations, the Hamiltonian system, and tether tension equations to derive multibody dynamic model. Consideration is also given to torque constraints at the tether's initiation and culmination points, with viable trajectories delineated. Additionally, novel control methodologies for mitigating tether oscillations through length extension and positional adjustments at the tether's starting point are elucidated. The validation of the proposed algorithms and trajectory planning strategies is conducted through dynamic simulations and verification processes utilizing MATLAB and Simulink software. Finally, recommendations for enhancing this space system and avenues for future research are proposed based on the findings and analyses presented."
    },
